{
  "name": "old-money-editorial-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Old Money/Quiet Luxury Editorial design system - an understated-wealth aesthetic built on a cream-linen-and-navy two-tone palette with gold used as a single rare hairline-rule flourish, a restrained moderate serif type scale (never oversized), outline buttons as the default register, and a simple monogram/crest mark - for integrating this refined, heritage, 'confidence through restraint' style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Old Money / Quiet Luxury Editorial\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to fashion or hospitality products; the understated-wealth aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: Wealth that doesn't announce itself — the visual language of a private members' club, a heritage tailor's fitting room, a sailing-club burgee, a hand-engraved invitation. This is luxury expressed through restraint, fine materials implied rather than illustrated, and a near-total absence of loud branding. If it has to shout to be noticed, it isn't old money.\n\n**Vibe**: Understated, refined, heritage, confident-without-effort, timeless. Think a Ralph Lauren catalog, a private bank's client portal, a tennis-club scorecard — never ornate (that's Academia's library warmth or Art Deco's geometric glamour), never stark (that's Monochrome's confrontational drama).\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"One quiet flourish, not ten.\" Where most luxury-adjacent styles reach for ornament (a monogram, a hairline rule, a small crest), this style permits exactly one at a time, used with total consistency, never layered or repeated within the same view. Confidence comes from what is *withheld*, not what is added.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Academia/Classical**: Academia is warm, textured, ornamented luxury (mahogany, brass, drop caps, arch-top imagery) evoking a library; Old Money is quieter and flatter — cream and navy, a single thin gold rule, moderate type scale, evoking fine stationery rather than an illuminated manuscript.\n- vs. **Minimalist Monochrome**: Monochrome is stark black/white with oversized 8-9xl serif drama, confrontational and editorial-loud; Old Money is soft cream/navy, moderate scale (never above ~text-6xl), and quiet rather than dramatic.\n- vs. **Art Deco/Gatsby**: Art Deco is geometric, symmetrical, and visibly ornamented (sunbursts, stepped frames); Old Money has almost no geometric ornament at all — its luxury signal is restraint and material/color quality, not pattern.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nA soft, muted palette evoking cream linen, navy blazer wool, and the faintest hint of gold thread. Use these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n*   `background`: `#FAF8F3` (Cream Linen)\n*   `surface`: `#F3EFE5` (Parchment) — cards, one step up from background\n*   `foreground`: `#1B2A41` (Navy Ink) — the primary text and structural color, used the way most styles use black\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#6B6558` (Warm Taupe Gray)\n*   `accent` (Thin Gold): `#A67C3D` — used only for a single hairline rule, a monogram, or a small label per view; never a fill color for large areas\n*   `secondary` (Deep Forest, optional): `#2F4538` — an alternative quiet accent for a product that wants a second cool tone instead of gold\n*   `border`: `rgba(27, 42, 65, 0.12)` (dim navy hairline)\n\n**Rule**: this is fundamentally a **cream + navy** two-tone system with gold as a rare third accent — never let gold cover more than a hairline rule or a small mark; it is punctuation, not paint.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Body/Display**: an elegant, quiet serif — **'Freight Text'**, or the widely-available **'Lora'** or **'Georgia'** — for both body copy and headlines, keeping the type family singular (no separate \"display font\") for a coherent, understated voice.\n*   **Labels**: small caps or a light-tracked uppercase (`tracking-[0.15em]`, `text-xs`) in `muted-foreground` — the one place this style borrows a formal, stationery-like convention.\n*   **Scale**: moderate throughout — hero headlines cap around `text-5xl` to `text-6xl` (never the 8-9xl drama of Monochrome), section titles `text-3xl` to `text-4xl`, body `text-base` to `text-lg` with generous `leading-relaxed`.\n*   **Weight**: Regular (400) for body, Medium (500) for headings — avoid Bold/Black weights, which read as assertive rather than quietly confident.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Radius**: modest and consistent — `rounded-md` (6px) to `rounded-lg` (8px) for buttons and inputs; cards can be `rounded-none` to `rounded-md` (a very slight rounding at most) — this style sits between Monochrome's zero-radius austerity and softer systems' generous curves.\n*   **No decorative shapes, no blobs, no geometric motifs** — the restraint that defines other parts of the system extends to shape as well.\n\n### Borders, Rules & the One Gold Flourish\n*   **Hairline navy rules** (`1px solid border`) divide sections — used generously, quietly, like the ruled lines in fine stationery.\n*   **The single gold rule**: exactly one `1-2px solid accent` (gold) rule per view, placed beneath the single most important heading or above a primary CTA — its rarity is the entire point.\n*   **Monogram/Crest mark**: a small, simple initial-based mark (a circle or shield containing 1-2 letters) in `foreground` or a thin `accent` outline — used as the logo, never elaborated into full ornament.\n*   **No shadows beyond the faintest card lift** (`0 1px 2px rgba(27,42,65,0.05)`) — depth is not this style's tool; alignment and margin are.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Primary**: `bg-foreground` (navy) fill, `background` (cream) text, `rounded-md`, no shadow.\n*   **Secondary/Outline (most common)**: `bg-transparent`, `border border-foreground`, `foreground` text — outline buttons are the default register for this style; a filled button is reserved for the one primary action per view.\n*   **Typography**: small caps or light-tracked uppercase, `text-sm`, moderate padding — never oversized.\n*   **Interaction**: quiet — a slow, subtle background fade on hover (`duration-300 ease-out`), no lift, no scale.\n\n### Cards / Containers\n*   **Structure**: `surface` fill, hairline `border`, minimal-to-no radius, faint shadow at most.\n*   **Content**: generous margins inside the card — this style, like Scandinavian, trusts whitespace over chrome, but keeps a warmer cream/navy palette and serif voice.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `background` fill, `border-b border-foreground/30` (bottom-border, stationery-line convention) or a full hairline box — `rounded-md`.\n*   **Focus**: border shifts to full-opacity `foreground` (navy) — a quiet, structural change, no glow, no color shift to gold (gold stays reserved for its rare structural role).\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: centered or flush-left wordmark, generously spaced nav links in small caps, a single hairline `border-b` beneath the entire bar — no background fill distinguishing it from the page.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-5xl` to `max-w-6xl` — more intimate than most systems in this collection, echoing a letter's margins rather than a poster's bleed.\n*   **Margins**: generous and consistent, like a fine stationery layout — err toward more whitespace than feels necessary.\n*   **Section Rhythm**: `py-20` to `py-28`, divided by hairline navy rules rather than background-color shifts.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **The single gold rule** — used exactly once per view, never repeated or layered.\n*   **Monogram/crest mark** as the entire logo treatment — no elaborate emblem.\n*   **Moderate, never-oversized type scale** — the clearest signal that this is restraint, not a lesser version of Monochrome's drama.\n*   **Outline buttons as the default**, filled reserved for the one primary action.\n*   **Two-tone cream/navy base** with gold strictly as punctuation.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: slow and quiet — `duration-300` to `duration-400`, simple `ease-out`, no bounce, no glow, no dramatic reveal.\n*   **Minimal motion overall** — confidence is expressed through stillness.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: sparse, thin-stroke (1.5px) line icons in `foreground`, used only where functionally necessary — this style, like Swiss, avoids decorative iconography.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: navy-on-cream clears AAA comfortably; verify `accent` gold against `background` for the rare instances it carries text (generally fine for a label but should not carry body copy).\n*   **Focus States**: a clear navy border/ring — quiet in color but never subtle in visibility for keyboard users.\n*   **Line Length**: comfortable 60-75 character measure, consistent with the serif body type's readability needs.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: margins compress proportionally but stay generous relative to content; the single gold rule and monogram remain present at every size — they're identity, not decoration to cut first.\n*   **Typography**: modest step-down (`text-5xl` → `text-4xl` → `text-3xl`) rather than dramatic multi-step scaling.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Marketing / hospitality / private-club / heritage-brand sites**: the most natural home — quiet hero, single gold rule, monogram mark.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools (esp. private banking, wealth management, legal)**: the navy/cream palette and hairline-rule structure read as trustworthy and serious for finance/legal-adjacent products; keep gold reserved for a single \"premium tier\" signal rather than scattering it through the UI.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: understated product photography framing, outline \"Add to Bag\" buttons, monogram as a watermark.\n*   **Mobile apps**: centered monogram splash screen, outline buttons throughout, single gold rule reserved for a key confirmation screen.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill old-money-editorial-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
